.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
The SSH transport layer (RFC 4253) as a state machine: identification exchange, algorithm negotiation, key exchange, key derivation, the binary packet protocol, rekeying, and the transport messages (DISCONNECT, IGNORE, UNIMPLEMENTED, DEBUG, SERVICE_REQUEST, SERVICE_ACCEPT, EXT_INFO).
.PP
Like everything else here it does no I/O and never blocks.  The caller hands it the bytes it reads from its socket with gf_conn_receive, and the connection calls back with the bytes to write.  Time is a caller\-supplied monotonic clock in MICROSECONDS, passed to every call that can need it, so the library behaves identically under a simulated clock.  gf_conn_timeout says when to call gf_conn_on_timeout next.  All callbacks are invoked synchronously from inside the call that caused them, and must not free the connection (call gf_conn_disconnect and free it afterwards).
.PP
.nf
.RS
What it does for you:
  \- sends our identification and KEXINIT as soon as gf_conn_start is
    called, and answers the peer's;
  \- refuses anything the policy forbids, and requires strict key
    exchange (the Terrapin countermeasure; see kexinit.h);
  \- verifies the server's host key signature and asks the application
    (the host_key callback) whether it trusts the key: a client has no
    default and must set the callback;
  \- rekeys before the packet layer's limits and every hour or gigabyte
    (config below), whether we or the peer start it, once the layer above
    has said the user is authenticated (gf_conn_set_authenticated);
    application messages sent during a key exchange are held and sent
    after it;
  \- answers a peer's DISCONNECT, and sends the right DISCONNECT when it
    finds a protocol violation.
.RE
.fi
.PP
Layers above (authentication, channels) see only messages numbered 50 and up, through the message callback, and send with gf_conn_send. After a fatal error or a disconnect every call but gf_conn_free returns GF_ERR_CLOSED.
.PP
Threading: one connection is used from one thread at a time.
.SH "TYPES AND CONSTANTS"
.PP
.nf
enum gf_msg
{
  GF_MSG_DISCONNECT = 1,
  GF_MSG_IGNORE = 2,
  GF_MSG_UNIMPLEMENTED = 3,
  GF_MSG_DEBUG = 4,
  GF_MSG_SERVICE_REQUEST = 5,
  GF_MSG_SERVICE_ACCEPT = 6,
  GF_MSG_EXT_INFO = 7,
  GF_MSG_NEWKEYS = 21,
  GF_MSG_KEX_ECDH_INIT = 30,	/* Also SSH_MSG_KEX_HYBRID_INIT.  */
  GF_MSG_KEX_ECDH_REPLY = 31	/* Also SSH_MSG_KEX_HYBRID_REPLY.  */
};
.fi
.RS
Transport message numbers (RFC 4250 section 4.1.2).
.RE
.PP
.nf
enum gf_disconnect
{
  GF_DISCONNECT_HOST_NOT_ALLOWED = 1,
  GF_DISCONNECT_PROTOCOL_ERROR = 2,
  GF_DISCONNECT_KEY_EXCHANGE_FAILED = 3,
  GF_DISCONNECT_RESERVED = 4,
  GF_DISCONNECT_MAC_ERROR = 5,
  GF_DISCONNECT_COMPRESSION_ERROR = 6,
  GF_DISCONNECT_SERVICE_NOT_AVAILABLE = 7,
  GF_DISCONNECT_PROTOCOL_VERSION_NOT_SUPPORTED = 8,
  GF_DISCONNECT_HOST_KEY_NOT_VERIFIABLE = 9,
  GF_DISCONNECT_CONNECTION_LOST = 10,
  GF_DISCONNECT_BY_APPLICATION = 11,
  GF_DISCONNECT_TOO_MANY_CONNECTIONS = 12,
  GF_DISCONNECT_AUTH_CANCELLED_BY_USER = 13,
  GF_DISCONNECT_NO_MORE_AUTH_METHODS = 14,
  GF_DISCONNECT_ILLEGAL_USER_NAME = 15
};
